.TH RIGOR 1 "2026" "rigor 0.9.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rigor \- opt-in dev environment helpers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rigor
.I command
.RI [ feature ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rigor
bundles small, independently enabled zsh helpers. Each feature is a script
you source from your
.IR ~/.zshrc ;
enable some, all, or none.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B enable " [feature...]"
Enable feature(s) by appending a source line to your
.IR ~/.zshrc .
Idempotent. With no feature, enables everything. Run
.B exec zsh
afterward to apply to the current shell.
.RB ( install
is accepted as an alias.)
.TP
.B disable " <feature|all>"
Disable a feature by removing its line from your
.IR ~/.zshrc .
A feature (or
.BR all )
is required \(em a bare
.B disable
will not silently remove everything.
.RB ( uninstall
is accepted as an alias.)
.TP
.B status
Show which features are currently enabled.
.TP
.B doctor
Report the resolved python/pip and the active virtualenv.
.TP
.B init " [feature...]"
Print the raw source line(s) for manual use or
.BR eval .
.TP
.B sleep " on|off|status"
.B off
runs
.BR "sudo pmset -a disablesleep 1" ,
.B on
runs
.BR "sudo pmset -a disablesleep 0" ,
.B status
reports which (no
.B sudo
needed for
.BR status ).
A memorable name for a command whose flag/argument order is easy to forget.
.TP
.B push " setup|status|test|off"
Notify your phone when a Claude Code or Codex turn ends, over
.BR ntfy (https://ntfy.sh).
.B setup
wires this machine and prints the topic to subscribe to; pass
.B \-\-topic
to join the topic your other machines already use,
.B \-\-device
to label this one \(em or
.B \-\-device
with an empty string to leave this machine out of the notification titles
altogether, which is worth doing when you only run one \(em and
.B \-\-server
to use your own ntfy instance.
.B status
reports what is wired and masks the topic unless given
.BR \-\-show ;
.B test
sends one notification and reads it back to prove delivery;
.B off
unwires and restores what was there before. Both
.B setup
and
.B off
back up every file they edit
.RB ( .rigor-bak )
and are safe to re-run. Claude Code notifies at the end of a turn and whenever
it is blocked on you; Codex notifies at the end of a turn. Turns shorter than
.B RIGOR_PUSH_MIN_SECONDS
stay quiet, except that a turn ending in a question always notifies, at high
priority \(em the threshold exists to skip turns you never walked away from, and
a question means the agent is stopped and waiting however fast it got there.
Notification types that do not mean "waiting on you" \(em
.BR auth_success ,
.BR agent_completed ,
quota auto-resume \(em are dropped. Titles read
.IR "project \(bu agent @ machine" ,
most-identifying part first, because a phone truncates a title to roughly one
line and the project is the part worth keeping. A Codex turn whose whole answer is a JSON document is not forwarded: the
ChatGPT desktop app runs background turns of its own that answer that way, and
they are not worth an interruption. Codex permits a single
.B notify
program, so if one is already configured \(em the ChatGPT desktop app installs
its own \(em rigor parks that command and replays it before pushing, and
.B off
hands the slot back.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RIGOR_RC
Override the rc file that enable/disable edit (default
.IR ~/.zshrc ).
.B DEVRIG_RC
and
.B EZENV_RC
are honored as deprecated fallbacks from earlier names of this tool.
.TP
.B RIGOR_SHIM_DIR
Override where pyf writes its python/pip shims.
.B DEVRIG_SHIM_DIR
and
.B EZENV_SHIM_DIR
are honored as deprecated fallbacks.
.TP
.B RIGOR_PUSH_ENV
Override the push config file (default
.IR ~/.config/rigor/push.env ).
.TP
.B RIGOR_PUSH_CLAUDE_SETTINGS
Override the Claude Code settings file that
.B push
edits (default
.IR ~/.claude/settings.json ).
.TP
.B RIGOR_PUSH_CODEX_CONF
Override the Codex config file that
.B push
edits (default
.IR ~/.codex/config.toml ).
.SH FEATURES
.TP
.B autovenv
Auto-activate the nearest
.I .venv
when you cd into a directory tree that contains one, and deactivate on leaving.
The current directory wins: leaving every
.I .venv
scope deactivates whatever is active, including a venv activated by your editor.
On
.BR "rigor enable autovenv" ,
it offers (with a prompt) to disable VSCode/Cursor's own terminal venv
auto-activation
.RB ( python.terminal.activateEnvironment )
in your user settings, so autovenv is the sole venv manager;
.B "rigor disable autovenv"
offers to undo it. Those edits are backed up alongside the file
.RB ( .rigor-bak ).
.TP
.B pyf
Create shims so bare
.B python
and
.B pip
resolve to python3/pip3, without shadowing virtualenvs or other interpreters.
.TP
.B envup
Add an
.B envup
shell function that exports a
.I .env
file into the current shell \(em
.B envup
loads
.IR ./.env ,
.B "envup FILE"
a specific one. Shorthand for
.BR "set -a; source <file>; set +a" .
It is a sourced function rather than a
.B rigor
subcommand because a subprocess cannot export variables back into your shell.
Named
.B envup
rather than
.B dotenv
to avoid shadowing the python-dotenv CLI.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <prefix>/share/rigor/*.zsh
The feature scripts you source.
.TP
.I <prefix>/share/rigor/push/
The notification transport and the agent hook scripts that
.B push
points Claude Code and Codex at.
.TP
.I ~/.config/rigor/push.env
Push config, mode 600. On public ntfy.sh the topic name in it is the only thing
protecting the feed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Enable features, then reload the shell to apply:
.PP
.nf
.RS
rigor enable autovenv       # per-repo .venv auto-activation
rigor enable pyf            # bare python/pip -> python3/pip3
rigor enable                # everything
exec zsh                    # apply to the current shell
.RE
.fi
.PP
Check and undo:
.PP
.nf
.RS
rigor status                # what's enabled
rigor disable pyf           # turn one off
.RE
.fi
.PP
Wire it up by hand instead (no
.IR ~/.zshrc
edit):
.PP
.nf
.RS
source "$(brew --prefix)/share/rigor/autovenv.zsh"
source "$(brew --prefix)/share/rigor/pyf.zsh"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Notify your phone when an agent turn ends, on this machine and then a second one:
.PP
.nf
.RS
rigor push setup                       # prints the topic to subscribe to
rigor push test                        # confirm it arrives
rigor push setup --topic TOPIC --device mini   # on the next machine
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR brew (1),
.BR curl (1)
